  "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=21\\,\\text{ft}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{14}{2}=7\\,\\text{ft}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the liquid level. We call $h$ the height of the liquid and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the liquid. Both of these quantities, and the liquid volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the liquid must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{7}{21} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (16)^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-56.9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the liquid volume decreases at a rate of approximately $56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$",
